Description: BPM column in the browse view
 The Rekordbox views showed a fixed set of #, Title, Artist, Key, Duration.
 BPM is the other number a DJ reads when picking the next track, and it was
 already imported from the PDB (rekordboxfeature.cpp binds :bpm from
 track->tempo()) and already present in the track source's column list -- it
 was simply never shown. The visible set becomes #, Title, Artist, BPM, Key,
 Duration, with BPM sized to content next to Key on the right.
 .
 Three things had to be fixed for the column to actually arrive and look
 right:
 .
 1. A column added to the set would not appear on any unit that had already
    run an earlier version. WTrackTableViewHeader::restoreHeaderState() hides
    every section and then re-shows only those the saved state lists, and the
    saved state (persisted in mixxxdb.sqlite via SettingsDAO, so it survives
    reinstalls of the deb) records BPM as hidden from when it was internal.
    WTrackTableView::setTrackModel() now re-shows every non-internal column
    for a model with fixedColumnLayout(): for those models isColumnInternal()
    *is* the visible set and has to win over restored state. This fixes the
    whole class of bug, not just this column -- every future change to the set
    would otherwise be invisible on exactly the units that already have the
    product installed.
 .
 2. Every BPM cell would have drawn an indeterminate BPM-lock indicator. The
    Rekordbox table has no bpm_lock column, so rawSiblingValue() returns null
    for it and BaseTrackTableModel::data() fell through to PartiallyChecked.
    A model without the lock column has no lock state to report and now
    returns no check state at all.
 .
 3. BPMDelegate is only installed when the model has a bpm_lock column. The
    delegate exists to draw and toggle that indicator, and it paints the cell
    through a hidden QCheckBox named LibraryBPMButton as the style source --
    which the skin does not style, so the cell would not have matched the rest
    of the list. Without the delegate the BPM cell renders through the view's
    default path, like Title or Duration. Nothing is lost on the Rekordbox
    views: BaseExternalPlaylistModel::flags() returns readOnlyFlags(), so the
    cell was never editable or checkable there anyway, and the displayed value
    comes from the model's DisplayRole (locale-formatted to the
    Preferences > Library BPM precision), not from the delegate.
 .
 Sorting by BPM needed no change: RekordboxPlaylistModel::initSortColumnMapping
 already maps SortColumnId::Bpm, and the list still opens sorted by # because
 forceDefaultSort() is unaffected.
Author: Pioneered CI <pioneered-build@localhost>
Forwarded: no

